WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.118
The main theorem shows that a number of iterations,
and a matching number of samples,

00:00:05.198 --> 00:00:10.376
that grow only polynomially in the problem
parameters is enough to drive the in-domain

00:00:10.456 --> 00:00:13.006
generalization error down to order epsilon.

00:00:13.086 --> 00:00:18.020
Crucially, the same trained model also generalizes
out of domain to shifted tasks,

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:23.217
as long as the new relevant patterns are linear
combinations of the training patterns

00:00:23.297 --> 00:00:25.786
with coefficients summing to at least one.

00:00:25.866 --> 00:00:31.167
The experiments confirm this sharply: the
out-of-domain classification error falls below

00:00:31.247 --> 00:00:35.325
one percent exactly when the combination strength
S-one reaches one,

00:00:35.405 --> 00:00:37.098
and stays near zero above it,

00:00:37.178 --> 00:00:42.540
while the required context length and iterations
track the predicted dependence on alpha.
